---
import DocLayout from "../../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import { routeFor } from "../../i18n";
---

<DocLayout
  lang="en"
  page="docs/storage"
  title="Storage architecture"
  intro="The workspace keeps durable content as local files and treats indexes, cache, and app state as rebuildable support data."
  next={[
    { href: routeFor("en", "privacy"), label: "Data and privacy" },
    { href: routeFor("en", "docs/links"), label: "Link rules" },
  ]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>Local-first shape</h2>
    <p>A typical workspace has three important areas: <code>notes/</code> for HTML documents, <code>assets/</code> for images and attachments, and <code>.opaline/</code> for metadata, settings, indexes, cache, and history.</p>
    <p>The user-owned HTML files are the source of truth. Opaline should prefer rebuilding derived data from those files whenever possible.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What SQLite is for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Metadata and app state.</li>
      <li>Search indexes and relationship indexes.</li>
      <li>Backlinks, tags, recent notes, and favorites.</li>
      <li>Embeddings and cache when those features are enabled.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Extensions</h2>
    <p>Extension widgets should work through controlled APIs rather than taking direct ownership of workspace storage. That keeps platform-specific access behind adapters and preserves user trust.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Practical rule</h2>
    <p>If the app disappears, the documents should still be useful. If a derived database disappears, Opaline should be able to rebuild enough state from the workspace to keep going.</p>
  </section>
</DocLayout>
